 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Compression ratio",
   "expr": "ΔVOUT(dB) = ΔVIN(dB) / r, for VDE < VIN < VRP",
   "where": {
    "r": "compression ratio, 1 to 10, set by RCOMP",
    "VDE": "downward expansion (noise gate) threshold",
    "VRP": "rotation point, 63 mV rms"
   },
   "note": "Above VRP the ratio is fixed near 10:1 whatever RCOMP is; below VDE a 1 dB fall at the input gives about a 3 dB fall at the output.",
   "page": 8
  },
  {
   "name": "RMS detector time constant",
   "expr": "τ ≈ 10 × CAVG",
   "where": {
    "τ": "detector filter time constant, ms",
    "CAVG": "capacitor on AVG CAP, µF"
   },
   "note": "τ governs both the steady averaging and the compression release time.",
   "page": 9
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Transfer characteristic",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The output-against-input curve in dB has three regions. Below the downward expansion threshold VDE the part gates: each 1 dB the input drops lowers the output by about 3 dB, so very quiet inputs get little gain. Between VDE and the rotation point VRP the output changes by 1 dB for every r dB at the input, r being the compression ratio. Above VRP the part limits at a fixed ratio of roughly 10:1, independent of RCOMP. The gain at an input of VRP is the 18 dB fixed gain at every compression ratio, so changing r pivots the compression line about that point."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Signal path",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "INPUT feeds a unity-gain buffer of roughly 100 kΩ input impedance whose input rests near 400 mV dc; a ground-referenced source therefore connects through a series capacitor, 0.1 µF for most audio. The buffer output drives both the rms detector and, through an external capacitor C2 from BUFOUT to VCAIN, the VCA. C2 works into the 1 kΩ VCA input, which fixes its typical value at 4.7 µF to 10 µF (aluminium electrolytic is the low-cost choice) and, with it, the lower -3 dB frequency. The VCA converts the signal current in C2 to a voltage at OUTPUT (pin 9); overall gain reaches 40 dB, depending on the control circuitry. OUTPUT has a typical impedance below 145 Ω, wants a load above 5 kΩ and rests near 1.4 V dc, so a grounded load needs a series capacitor."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Bandwidth and noise",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The upper -3 dB point stays above 1 MHz at gains up to 30 dB. The buffer's noise is fixed; the VCA's input-referred noise depends on gain and is lowest at maximum gain, which extends usable dynamic range."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Level detector",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "A full-wave rectifier feeds a true-rms detector whose averaging time constant comes from CAVG on pin 6. CAVG = 2.2 µF keeps the detector working down to 10 Hz; a larger value can cut the response to heavy low-frequency ambient noise. Electrolytics of 2 µF to 22 µF are the recommended range. An undersized CAVG can make some signals pump; an oversized one slows the response to level changes. Attack (gain falling after the input rises) is governed mainly by internal circuitry that accelerates for large steps, so overload usually lasts under 35 ms. Attack depends only on CAVG; release is a linear ramp in dB whose duration depends on CAVG and on step size, at about 240 dB/s with 2.2 µF and 12 dB/s with 22 µF."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Control circuitry",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The detector output is proportional to the logarithm of the rms level plus a dc offset. The control block subtracts a dc voltage, scales the result and drives the VCA, whose gain in dB is linear in its control signal; this is what makes the compression line straight on a dB plot. The scaling, set by RCOMP, is the compression ratio."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Setting the compression ratio",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "RCOMP from pin 8 to VDD sets r; a lower RCOMP gives a lower ratio. Shorting pin 8 to VDD turns compression off (1:1). AGC behaviour comes with ratios of 2:1 to 10:1, depending on the application. When a resistor is used it should be above 5 kΩ: a smaller value may be taken as a short, 0 Ω."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Setting the noise gate threshold",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "RGATE from pin 7 to VDD sets the downward expansion threshold between -40 dBV and -55 dBV; a higher resistance lowers the threshold. 0 Ω gives about 10 mV rms (-40 dBV) and 5 kΩ about 1 mV rms (-55 dBV). RGATE above 5 kΩ is not recommended: the part's own noise floor stops the gate working properly any lower."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Rotation point",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The rotation point is fixed internally at -24 dBV (63 mV rms). Inputs above it are limited, capping the output so a following codec or ADC is not driven into clipping."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Shutdown",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "SHUTDOWN (pin 3) is active low and CMOS compatible: 0 V on it brings supply current below 10 µA, typically below 2 µA, and leaves INPUT and OUTPUT high impedance, their voltages set by the external circuit. Settling times: about 200 ms from shutdown back to operation, and typically under 1 s into shutdown. Cycling the supply is faster, under 200 ms off to on and under 1 ms on to off. Shutdown current varies with temperature and supply voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Layout",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The part has wide bandwidth and up to 60 dB of gain, so capacitive coupling from output back to input must be kept low; input and output traces should not run next to each other. Recommended: a star (single-point) ground, short leads and short traces, and, where analog and digital grounds are separate, the part and its components on analog ground. Wire-wrap boards and similar grounding are to be avoided."
  }
 ],
